1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,520
Imagine deciding to live without a car.

2
00:00:02,740 --> 00:00:05,900
Without television. Without computers.

3
00:00:06,540 --> 00:00:09,460
Without a connection
to the public electricity grid.

4
00:00:09,800 --> 00:00:13,580
And imagine your children leaving school
after the eighth grade.

5
00:00:14,247 --> 00:00:17,087
Sounds like some kind
of dystopian religious cult?

6
00:00:17,667 --> 00:00:19,167
It might seem that way.

7
00:00:19,707 --> 00:00:21,427
But the Amish aren't a cult.

8
00:00:22,133 --> 00:00:23,933
Here's the surprising part.

9
00:00:24,013 --> 00:00:26,633
The Amish are not actually
against technology.

10
00:00:26,863 --> 00:00:31,753
And they're not simply trying to recreate
life in the eighteenth century.

11
00:00:32,345 --> 00:00:34,165
In fact,
their relationship with technology

12
00:00:34,395 --> 00:00:37,045
is much more interesting than that.

13
00:00:37,405 --> 00:00:41,945
They ask a different question: What
will this technology do to our community?

14
00:00:43,347 --> 00:00:46,207
The Amish are Christian
Anabaptist communities.

15
00:00:46,927 --> 00:00:49,687
Their roots go back
to sixteenth-century Europe.

16
00:00:50,357 --> 00:00:54,517
They emerged as a distinct religious
movement in the late seventeenth century,

17
00:00:54,597 --> 00:00:58,242
following a split
associated with Jakob Ammann.

18
00:00:58,567 --> 00:01:01,917
Today there are more than
four hundred and twenty thousand of them,

19
00:01:01,997 --> 00:01:04,977
almost all of them
in the United States and Canada.

20
00:01:05,623 --> 00:01:09,223
Among themselves,
most Amish speak Pennsylvania German,

21
00:01:09,303 --> 00:01:11,563
often called Pennsylvania Dutch.

22
00:01:12,313 --> 00:01:16,943
Children learn English at school,
and the Bible is read in standard German.

23
00:01:17,519 --> 00:01:20,519
Each Amish community follows
its own set of rules,

24
00:01:20,679 --> 00:01:23,599
usually unwritten, known as the Ordnung.

25
00:01:23,899 --> 00:01:26,459
These rules govern everyday life,

26
00:01:26,539 --> 00:01:28,839
including which technologies
are acceptable.

27
00:01:29,370 --> 00:01:32,050
And because different communities
have different rules,

28
00:01:32,250 --> 00:01:33,870
there is no single universal list

29
00:01:34,100 --> 00:01:37,490
of technologies that all Amish people
use or reject.

30
00:01:39,570 --> 00:01:42,990
Let's start with something familiar. Cars.

31
00:01:43,150 --> 00:01:45,830
Why do many Amish communities
prohibit their members

32
00:01:46,060 --> 00:01:47,970
from owning and driving cars?

33
00:01:48,694 --> 00:01:52,174
It's not because they don't understand
combustion engines.

34
00:01:52,614 --> 00:01:57,554
Amish people can travel by car,
but they often rely on non-Amish drivers.

35
00:01:58,134 --> 00:02:00,614
They can also use buses and trains.

36
00:02:01,445 --> 00:02:04,705
The restriction is primarily about
owning and driving their own vehicles.

37
00:02:04,935 --> 00:02:06,135
Why?

38
00:02:06,951 --> 00:02:10,271
Because a car is more than just
a means of transportation.

39
00:02:11,211 --> 00:02:15,231
It allows you to travel dozens of miles
in a relatively short time.

40
00:02:15,839 --> 00:02:17,979
You can live far away from your family.

41
00:02:18,199 --> 00:02:19,739
Work somewhere else.

42
00:02:19,819 --> 00:02:21,959
Spend your evenings in another town.

43
00:02:22,189 --> 00:02:25,779
And gradually,
the community becomes less connected.

44
00:02:26,457 --> 00:02:29,797
So the problem isn't necessarily
that cars are too modern.

45
00:02:30,497 --> 00:02:34,277
It's that cars can fundamentally change
how the community functions.

46
00:02:37,057 --> 00:02:38,917
Many traditional Amish communities

47
00:02:39,147 --> 00:02:42,497
reject connections
to the public electricity grid.

48
00:02:42,897 --> 00:02:45,737
But that doesn't mean
they never use electricity.

49
00:02:46,670 --> 00:02:50,050
Some use batteries, generators,
solar panels,

50
00:02:50,130 --> 00:02:52,950
pneumatic systems,
and other sources of power.

51
00:02:53,810 --> 00:02:58,410
You can even find Amish workshops equipped
with surprisingly sophisticated machinery.

52
00:02:59,163 --> 00:03:01,923
The important distinction is
that electricity itself

53
00:03:02,153 --> 00:03:03,343
isn't necessarily forbidden.

54
00:03:03,573 --> 00:03:08,663
Dependence on the outside infrastructure
can be the real concern.

55
00:03:09,283 --> 00:03:11,063
That's a very different philosophy.

56
00:03:13,633 --> 00:03:16,093
Again, there is no universal answer.

57
00:03:16,853 --> 00:03:19,653
Some Amish communities
restrict agricultural machinery

58
00:03:19,883 --> 00:03:22,773
and continue to rely heavily on horses.

59
00:03:23,853 --> 00:03:26,793
Others are considerably
more technologically permissive.

60
00:03:27,535 --> 00:03:30,995
Some use modified tractors,
modern woodworking equipment,

61
00:03:31,075 --> 00:03:34,795
chainsaws, pneumatic tools,
and various industrial machines.

62
00:03:35,246 --> 00:03:39,066
So this isn't a society
where everything has to be made by hand.

63
00:03:39,446 --> 00:03:40,926
Far from it.

64
00:03:41,688 --> 00:03:43,708
A chainsaw makes work easier.

65
00:03:44,468 --> 00:03:47,328
A car can change where people go,
how far apart they live,

66
00:03:47,558 --> 00:03:49,648
and how often they see one another.

67
00:03:52,538 --> 00:03:55,598
Some Amish families once had
telephones at home.

68
00:03:56,398 --> 00:03:59,658
Around 1910, the Lancaster Amish
banned them.

69
00:04:00,138 --> 00:04:03,298
Later, many communities
let the telephone back in.

70
00:04:03,378 --> 00:04:05,298
But not into the kitchen.

71
00:04:05,973 --> 00:04:08,093
In some communities, the solution was

72
00:04:08,323 --> 00:04:11,133
a small wooden telephone booth
outside the home.

73
00:04:11,873 --> 00:04:14,273
Sometimes several families shared one.

74
00:04:14,818 --> 00:04:17,498
The telephone was available
when it was needed.

75
00:04:18,178 --> 00:04:20,938
But it could not interrupt
every family meal.

76
00:04:21,058 --> 00:04:24,318
And it did not make visiting
your neighbours unnecessary.

77
00:04:25,158 --> 00:04:26,538
That is the difference.

78
00:04:27,318 --> 00:04:30,618
The question was never simply
whether a telephone was useful.

79
00:04:31,238 --> 00:04:33,958
It was what putting one inside the home
would change.

80
00:04:35,173 --> 00:04:39,033
Today, the same debate is back,
this time with mobile phones.

81
00:04:39,493 --> 00:04:42,613
Some communities allow them,
especially for business.

82
00:04:43,693 --> 00:04:44,993
Others restrict them.

83
00:04:45,573 --> 00:04:49,033
And even where they're accepted,
many try to keep them out of the home.

84
00:04:49,632 --> 00:04:51,592
The same logic applies to medicine.

85
00:04:52,532 --> 00:04:57,072
Their faith has no rule against doctors,
surgery, modern drugs or vaccines.

86
00:04:57,992 --> 00:05:01,172
When seriously ill,
many Amish go to the hospital.

87
00:05:01,672 --> 00:05:05,372
Still, in some communities,
many children are not vaccinated.

88
00:05:06,267 --> 00:05:08,947
Many Amish avoid commercial insurance.

89
00:05:09,587 --> 00:05:13,967
Large hospital bills are often covered
through mutual aid within the community.

90
00:05:14,547 --> 00:05:18,267
They prefer to rely on each other
rather than on outside insurance.

91
00:05:19,247 --> 00:05:23,147
In 1965, Congress created
a Social Security exemption

92
00:05:23,377 --> 00:05:25,447
for qualifying religious groups.

93
00:05:26,127 --> 00:05:30,787
Those who qualify can pay nothing in,
but must give up any claim to benefits.

94
00:05:31,587 --> 00:05:35,747
Otherwise, they follow the law
and pay income, property and sales taxes.

95
00:05:36,687 --> 00:05:39,787
Where the law clashes with their faith,
they seek exceptions.

96
00:05:41,047 --> 00:05:46,107
In 1972, the US Supreme Court ruled
that states could not force Amish children

97
00:05:46,337 --> 00:05:48,747
to stay in school past the eighth grade.

98
00:05:49,227 --> 00:05:51,907
And their faith forbids military service.

99
00:05:54,153 --> 00:05:57,273
Now let's talk about one of the most
famous aspects of Amish life.

100
00:05:57,503 --> 00:05:58,703
Rumspringa.

101
00:06:00,438 --> 00:06:04,958
Around the age of sixteen, young Amish
people generally gain more freedom.

102
00:06:05,698 --> 00:06:09,838
This doesn't mean they suddenly receive
permission to do absolutely anything.

103
00:06:10,220 --> 00:06:14,400
But they have more opportunities to
experience life outside their community.

104
00:06:15,240 --> 00:06:17,120
Most of them still live at home.

105
00:06:17,720 --> 00:06:20,560
Some continue living
much as they always have.

106
00:06:21,179 --> 00:06:23,359
Others experiment with the outside world.

107
00:06:23,439 --> 00:06:28,759
Modern clothes. Cars. Movies. Television.

108
00:06:29,379 --> 00:06:33,059
And sometimes things that would normally
be unacceptable in their community.

109
00:06:33,869 --> 00:06:36,409
Eventually,
they face an important decision.

110
00:06:37,269 --> 00:06:40,689
Will they be baptized and become
full members of the Amish church?

111
00:06:40,769 --> 00:06:42,689
Or will they leave?

112
00:06:43,334 --> 00:06:45,774
A large majority decide to stay.

113
00:06:46,614 --> 00:06:49,594
According to the Young Center
for Anabaptist and Pietist Studies,

114
00:06:49,824 --> 00:06:53,954
eighty-five per cent or more
of Amish youth join the church.

115
00:06:54,960 --> 00:06:57,060
So why do so many of them stay?

116
00:06:58,387 --> 00:07:00,227
There is no single explanation.

117
00:07:00,767 --> 00:07:02,347
But imagine the difference.

118
00:07:02,847 --> 00:07:05,947
Modern society offers:
Enormous personal freedom.

119
00:07:06,567 --> 00:07:11,727
Thousands of opportunities.
Travel. Career choices. Technology.

120
00:07:12,747 --> 00:07:13,947
Individual independence.

121
00:07:14,578 --> 00:07:17,278
The Amish community offers: Family.

122
00:07:17,378 --> 00:07:20,798
Strong social relationships. Clear rules.

123
00:07:21,098 --> 00:07:24,158
A familiar environment. Mutual assistance.

124
00:07:24,658 --> 00:07:26,478
A powerful sense of identity.

125
00:07:27,267 --> 00:07:29,987
It's a trade-off. Freedom for stability.

126
00:07:30,067 --> 00:07:32,487
Possibilities for certainty.

127
00:07:32,867 --> 00:07:34,647
Individualism for community.

128
00:07:35,217 --> 00:07:39,517
And for some people, the second side of
that equation is surprisingly attractive.

129
00:07:42,357 --> 00:07:44,377
It would be very easy to portray the Amish

130
00:07:44,607 --> 00:07:48,137
as happy people who escaped
a corrupted modern civilisation.

131
00:07:49,117 --> 00:07:51,077
But reality is more complicated.

132
00:07:51,948 --> 00:07:53,528
They have strict rules.

133
00:07:54,088 --> 00:07:56,228
Community pressure can be considerable.

134
00:07:56,908 --> 00:08:00,228
Formal education typically ends
after the eighth grade.

135
00:08:00,868 --> 00:08:03,468
Some communities
are extremely conservative.

136
00:08:04,038 --> 00:08:06,218
And for those who leave after baptism,

137
00:08:06,298 --> 00:08:09,938
it can mean being shunned
and losing much of their social world.

138
00:08:10,498 --> 00:08:12,478
Their religious system is not something

139
00:08:12,708 --> 00:08:15,678
that can simply be separated
from the rest of their lifestyle.

140
00:08:16,995 --> 00:08:18,335
This is important.

141
00:08:19,135 --> 00:08:22,675
Imagine taking the Amish way of life
and removing religion.

142
00:08:23,386 --> 00:08:25,826
You would still have:
Community cooperation.

143
00:08:25,906 --> 00:08:27,986
Restrictions on technology.

144
00:08:28,066 --> 00:08:29,646
Simple living.

145
00:08:29,726 --> 00:08:31,686
Strong family relationships.

146
00:08:32,168 --> 00:08:36,768
But you would lose the central mechanism
that holds much of the system together.

147
00:08:37,384 --> 00:08:42,504
So it's not entirely accurate to say: The
Amish simply chose a simpler lifestyle.

148
00:08:42,944 --> 00:08:45,224
They chose a particular
religious way of life,

149
00:08:45,454 --> 00:08:49,324
and limiting their exposure
to the outside world is part of it.

150
00:08:52,334 --> 00:08:56,074
The Amish did something
modern society often overlooks.

151
00:08:56,594 --> 00:09:00,374
They keep asking one question:
What will this do to our community?

152
00:09:01,200 --> 00:09:02,660
They accepted some technologies.

153
00:09:02,740 --> 00:09:04,760
Rejected others.

154
00:09:04,840 --> 00:09:07,820
And modified some
to fit their way of life.

155
00:09:08,867 --> 00:09:11,467
We don't have to follow
their example literally.

156
00:09:11,807 --> 00:09:14,967
But perhaps we could borrow
one simple idea.

157
00:09:15,557 --> 00:09:20,297
Not every technology we are capable of
creating is a technology we actually need.

158
00:09:20,797 --> 00:09:24,757
Technology should serve us.
Not the other way around.

159
00:09:25,691 --> 00:09:27,211
And if one day a washing machine

160
00:09:27,441 --> 00:09:30,151
needs an internet connection
and a monthly subscription

161
00:09:30,381 --> 00:09:32,291
just to wash your clothes...

162
00:09:32,731 --> 00:09:35,051
Perhaps it's time to establish
our own Ordnung.

163
00:09:36,785 --> 00:09:38,645
So here's a question for you.

164
00:09:39,265 --> 00:09:41,685
If you could keep one technology
out of your home,

165
00:09:41,765 --> 00:09:44,985
the way some Amish kept the telephone,
which one would it be?

166
00:09:45,625 --> 00:09:46,935
Let me know in the comments.
